"Other maps make the opposite choice. An equal-area projection keeps every size right, so Greenland shrinks back to one-fourteenth of Africa. The price is shape: on one well-known equal-area map, lands near the equator look tall and thin. Some people say equal-area maps are fairer for classrooms, because the Mercator map makes places far from the equator look larger than they are. Others answer that the Mercator map was built for steering ships and was never meant for comparing sizes. Each side is describing what its map does well.": "d9ae0898f3d4",
"Peel a tangerine in one piece, then try to press the peel flat on the table. It will not lie flat. It bulges, and if you push harder, it tears. Now think about the Earth. The Earth is round like the tangerine, and a map is flat like the table. Every flat map of the world has to solve the tangerine-peel problem somehow.": "e8acc243e489",
"Peel a tangerine in one piece, then try to press the peel flat on the table. It will not lie flat. It bulges, and if you push harder, it tears. Now think about the Earth. The Earth is round like the tangerine, and a map is flat like the table. Every flat map of the world has to solve the tangerine-peel problem somehow. A globe does not have this problem. A globe is a small model of the Earth with the same round shape, so the shapes of the lands, their sizes, the distances between places, and the directions from one place to another can all be right at the same time. But a globe is hard to carry, hard to print in a book, and it never shows you the whole world at once. So people flatten the globe. A map projection is a set of rules for moving every point on the round Earth to a point on a flat sheet. Here is the trouble: something has to stretch. In the 1820s the mathematician Carl Friedrich Gauss proved a theorem which shows that the surface of a ball cannot be flattened without changing some of its distances. Mapmakers call this stretching distortion. A flat map can keep shapes right, or keep areas right, or keep some distances or directions right, but it can never keep all of them right everywhere. The most famous choice was made in 1569 by Gerardus Mercator, who drew a map for sailors. On his map, a straight line always follows one compass direction, so a sailor could draw the route with a ruler. To make that work, the map stretches more and more toward the poles. At 60 degrees of latitude everything is drawn twice as wide and twice as tall, which means four times too large in area. That is why Greenland looks about as big as Africa on a Mercator map. In fact Africa is about fourteen times larger. Other maps make the opposite choice. An equal-area projection keeps every size right, so Greenland shrinks back to one-fourteenth of Africa. The price is shape: on one well-known equal-area map, lands near the equator look tall and thin. Some people say equal-area maps are fairer for classrooms, because the Mercator map makes places far from the equator look larger than they are. Others answer that the Mercator map was built for steering ships and was never meant for comparing sizes. Each side is describing what its map does well. So which map is the best? It depends on the job. To hold a compass course at sea, use a Mercator map. To compare the sizes of lands, use an equal-area map. To study the Arctic, use a map with the North Pole in the middle. To see everything right at once, pick up a globe. Every flat map is wrong somewhere, and a good map reader asks two questions: what was this map made for, and what did it give up?": "e0f2f75fb0a0",
